(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How common is basal cell carcinoma, and which ultraviolet exposure pattern drives it?","choices":["It is the fourth most common cancer overall, driven by intense intermittent ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people","It is a rare cancer, driven by human herpesvirus 8 in the setting of immune senescence","It is the most common form of cancer, driven by intense, INTERMITTENT ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people","It is the second most common skin cancer, driven by prolonged CUMULATIVE ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — intermittent, unlike the cumulative pattern of squamous cell carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which sites does the deck call high-risk for squamous cell carcinoma, and what does having more than ten tumours imply?","choices":["The palms, soles, nail units and periungual skin; more than ten tumours means higher rates of local recurrence and distant spread","Mucosal surfaces, lip, ear, scalp, temple, nose and genitalia; more than ten tumours means higher rates of local recurrence and nodal metastasis","The trunk, back, chest and shoulders; more than ten tumours means higher rates of local recurrence and distant nodal metastasis","Mucosal surfaces, lip, ear, scalp, temple and nose; more than ten tumours indicates indolent disease with a lower rate of recurrence and metastasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — site and tumour count both drive risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does Kaposi sarcoma look like, and which examination does the deck call essential when it is suspected?","choices":["Red or purple macules, plaques or nodules on skin or mucous membranes; ORAL examination is essential, since hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Red or purple macules, plaques or nodules on skin only; NAIL examination is essential, since subungual lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Pearly translucent papules with telangiectasias; ocular examination is essential, since eyelid lesions are the usual presenting site","Erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk; scalp examination is essential, since follicular involvement is the presenting sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the mouth may be where it declares itself. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How should a suspected basal cell carcinoma be sampled?","choices":["With a sentinel lymph node biopsy at the time of diagnosis","With a shave or punch biopsy","With a wide local excision carrying 1 cm margins","With a potassium hydroxide preparation of the surface scale"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the histologic subtype then dictates treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Describe the actinic keratosis lesion, including the finding the deck says may be more apparent by touch than by sight.","choices":["Small 0.2 to 0.6 cm pearly translucent or pink papules with visible telangiectasias that become more apparent when the skin is stretched","Small violaceous or purple macules and plaques, flesh-coloured early on, that are softer than surrounding skin and more apparent by sight than by touch","Small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation","Large 2 to 6 cm deeply pigmented plaques, flesh-coloured at the edge, with a smooth waxy stuck-on surface that is more apparent by sight than by touch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the sandpaper texture is often felt before it is seen. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the deck give for ADVANCED or metastatic basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Programmed death 1 blockade, or cetuximab","Intralesional vincristine, vinblastine or bleomycin","Topical corticosteroid with phototherapy and retinoids","Hedgehog pathway inhibitors — vismodegib or sonidegib"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — reserved for advanced disease or extensive tumour burden. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which initial skin-directed therapies does the deck list for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids, topical fluorouracil, imiquimod, and photodynamic therapy","Intralesional vincristine, vinblastine, bleomycin, and radiation","Liposomal doxorubicin, paclitaxel, romidepsin and vorinostat","Topical corticosteroids, topical mechlorethamine, bexarotene gel, and ultraviolet phototherapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — four skin-directed options. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the usual aetiology of squamous cell carcinoma, and which premalignant lesion may it arise from?","choices":["Prolonged cumulative sun exposure in fair-skinned people who burn easily; it may arise from an actinic keratosis","Intense intermittent sun exposure in fair-skinned people who tan easily; it may arise from a seborrheic keratosis","Prolonged cumulative sun exposure in darkly pigmented people; it may arise from a dermatofibroma","Chronic immunosuppression alone regardless of ultraviolet exposure; it may arise from a solar lentigo"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the continuum from actinic keratosis matters. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"When should a shave or punch biopsy be ordered for an actinic keratosis, and what must the interpretation distinguish?","choices":["Only when the lesion is cosmetically troublesome to the patient; the interpretation must confirm the depth of ultraviolet damage in the surrounding field","When morphology or behaviour raises concern for squamous cell carcinoma, or the lesion persists or recurs after therapy; the interpretation must separate actinic keratosis from carcinoma in situ from invasive carcinoma","Only after two failed courses of field-directed therapy, whatever the morphology or behaviour of the lesion; the interpretation need only confirm that some degree of keratinocytic dysplasia is present, since invasion does not alter treatment or margins","In every patient over 65 at the first visit regardless of behaviour; the interpretation must separate actinic keratosis from seborrheic keratosis and solar lentigo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — invasion changes treatment, margins and risk assessment substantially. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which lesion-directed therapy does the deck give for actinic keratosis, and what happens to the lesion afterwards?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy — the lesion blisters and resolves within 24 hours; preferred for widespread disease across a whole zone","Topical fluorouracil — the lesion crusts and disappears over 10 to 14 days; preferred for isolated lesions with clear borders","Surgical excision with 5 mm margins — the wound heals over 10 to 14 days; preferred for any lesion regardless of number","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy — the lesion crusts and disappears over 10 to 14 days; preferred for isolated or few lesions with clear borders"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lesion-directed, for small numbers. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which findings make referral URGENT in squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Only confirmed nodal disease, since high-risk site, size, recurrence and immune status can all be managed routinely","Only failure of two courses of topical therapy, whatever the site, size, histology, immune status or symptoms","High-risk site, size, recurrence, aggressive histology, immunosuppression, neurologic symptoms, or nodal disease","Cosmetic concern, patient anxiety, a lesion older than one year, a lesion on the trunk, or any pigmented lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — neurologic symptoms suggest perineural invasion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the deck say should be used to distinguish squamous cell carcinoma from its differential, rather than morphology alone?","choices":["Colour variegation, symmetry, border regularity, diameter and evolution alone","Patient age, sex, family history and occupational sun exposure alone","Dermoscopy pattern, Wood lamp fluorescence and surface scale alone","Time course, firmness or induration, ulceration, site, immune status and pathology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — six discriminators beyond appearance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is a typical actinic keratosis diagnosed?","choices":["Usually clinically, with dermoscopy supporting recognition when the clinician is trained","By shave or punch biopsy in every case, since clinical diagnosis is unreliable","By potassium hydroxide microscopy of the surface scale","By serology for ultraviolet-induced antibodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — biopsy is reserved for concerning or persistent lesions. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which entities does the deck list in the melanoma differential?","choices":["Psoriasis, drug eruption, photoallergy, eczematous dermatitis, syphilis, tinea corporis, follicular hair loss with erythroderma, and treatment-resistant patches or plaques","Dysplastic or changing naevus, seborrheic keratosis, solar lentigo, pigmented basal cell carcinoma, dermatofibroma, and amelanotic mimics","Actinic keratosis, carcinoma in situ, keratoacanthoma, verruca, inflamed seborrheic keratosis, basal cell carcinoma, amelanotic melanoma and chronic inflammatory or infectious ulcer","Bacillary angiomatosis, angioma or haemangioma, purpura and ecchymosis, venous stasis lesions, pyogenic granuloma, lymphoma, metastatic disease and other violaceous inflammatory lesions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the list also includes the lentigo maligna spectrum and two nail entries, subungual haematoma and benign longitudinal melanonychia. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which situations does the deck give as referral indications in actinic keratosis?","choices":["Only when the patient requests it for cosmetic reasons, since diagnostic uncertainty, high lesion burden and recurrence are all managed without referral","Diagnostic uncertainty or high lesion burden; recurrent or persistent lesions after appropriate therapy; immunosuppression, cosmetically sensitive sites, or concern for squamous cell carcinoma","Any lesion on the dorsal hand or forearm; any patient over the age of 50; any lesion larger than 3 mm; any lesion that has been present more than a year","Only after biopsy has already confirmed invasive squamous cell carcinoma, since uncertainty, lesion burden, recurrence and immunosuppression are all managed in primary care"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — three groupings. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which systemic agents are first-line in Kaposi sarcoma, and what does the deck say about combining them with antiretroviral therapy?","choices":["Romidepsin and vorinostat; antiretroviral therapy plus a histone deacetylase inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy plus chemotherapy is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone in advanced disease","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy alone is more effective than the combination in advanced disease","Vismodegib and sonidegib; antiretroviral therapy plus a hedgehog inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — combination beats antiretroviral therapy alone once disease is advanced. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What does the deck say about amputation in nail unit melanoma?","choices":["It is reserved for benign nail unit tumours such as glomus tumour or onychomatricoma that recur after local removal","It is NOT automatic — digit-sparing wide excision or Mohs with immunostaining is contemporary care, with amputation reserved for deep, extensive or bone-involving disease","It is the automatic first treatment for any nail unit melanoma whatever the depth or extent, since margins cannot be reliably assessed in the nail unit","It is never indicated at all, since digit-sparing wide excision clears every nail unit melanoma regardless of depth, extent or bone involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the digit is preserved where margins can be reliably assessed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Where does nail unit melanoma most often arise, which sites are high-yield, and is it ultraviolet-driven?","choices":["Most often in the matrix; the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites; it is NOT clearly ultraviolet-driven and may occur in any skin tone","Most often in the nail bed; the index finger and little toe are high-yield sites; it is clearly ultraviolet-driven and occurs mainly in fair skin","Most often in the proximal nail fold; all digits equally; it is clearly ultraviolet-driven and occurs mainly in fair skin","Most often in the hyponychium; the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites; it is driven by high-risk human papillomavirus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — delayed recognition contributes to advanced-stage presentation. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which factors does the deck list as amplifying actinic keratosis risk?","choices":["Obesity, diabetes with vascular disease, incontinence and immobility in a hospitalised patient, and recent antibiotic exposure","Atopic dermatitis, asthma and allergic rhinitis in a young adult, with a family history of atopy and frequent emollient use","Advanced age, cumulative ultraviolet exposure with outdoor work or recreation, prior actinic keratosis or keratinocyte carcinoma, male sex with bald scalp exposure, and immunosuppression","Young age, intermittent ultraviolet exposure with indoor occupation, no prior actinic keratosis or keratinocyte carcinoma, female sex with full scalp hair, and a fully competent immune system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — five amplifiers. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is mycosis fungoides, and how does it behave over time?","choices":["A cutaneous T-cell lymphoma that begins in skin and may remain confined there for years or decades, with malignant T cells forming skin infiltrates before any potential systemic spread","A cutaneous B-cell lymphoma that begins in the bone marrow and seeds the skin within weeks, with malignant B cells forming infiltrates only after systemic spread","A vascular tumour caused by human herpesvirus 8 that begins in the skin and spreads to the viscera within months of the first cutaneous lesion appearing","A keratinocyte carcinoma that begins as an actinic keratosis in sun-damaged skin and becomes invasive over years before any potential nodal spread"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — skin-limited for a long time in many patients. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the referral threshold for melanoma to an expert centre?","choices":["Melanoma deeper than 1 mm, or with lymph-node or other-site spread","Melanoma deeper than 4 mm only, since thinner disease is managed entirely in primary care","Any melanoma in a patient over 65, regardless of thickness or spread","Only melanoma that has already failed immunotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — confirmed melanoma warrants coordinated dermatology and surgical oncology care. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Where does melanoma rank among cancers in the United States, and what is its standing among skin diseases?","choices":["The 4th most common cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease","The most common cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease","The 4th most common cancer, but a rare cause of death compared with squamous cell carcinoma","The 2nd most common skin cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — incidence doubled over the preceding 30 years. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What treatment philosophy does the deck set out for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["A stage-directed, skin-first approach for most early disease, because early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure or prevent progression and may cause complications and premature death","Immediate aggressive systemic chemotherapy for all stages regardless of extent, because early aggressive treatment reliably cures the disease and prevents progression to systemic involvement","Observation alone at every stage without skin-directed treatment, because no therapy has been shown to alter the course of the disease or to relieve symptoms","Total-skin electron-beam treatment as the initial therapy for every patient regardless of stage, because skin-directed topical therapy has no demonstrated benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — aggression is a harm here, not a virtue. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which history elements does the deck ask you to elicit?","choices":["Onset and change; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars and radiation sites; family history — but not immunosuppression or transplant status, which do not alter cutaneous malignancy risk","Onset and change, pain or pruritus; occupational and recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; immunosuppression and family history — but not prior skin cancer, which does not predict a second primary","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational or recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; prior skin cancer, immunosuppression or transplant, human immunodeficiency virus risk; chronic scars, wounds or radiation sites; family history","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars, wounds and radiation sites; family history — but not prior skin cancer, immunosuppression, transplant or human immunodeficiency virus status, which the dermatologist obtains at the time of biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — note chronic scars and radiation sites among them. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which is the MOST COMMON malignant nail tumour, and what is it associated with?","choices":["Nail unit basal cell carcinoma, associated with cumulative ultraviolet exposure, fair skin, chronic trauma, prior radiation and older age","Glomus tumour, associated with chronic inflammation or trauma, immunosuppression, prior radiation and older age","Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma or Bowen disease, associated with high-risk human papillomavirus, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation or trauma, prior radiation, and older age","Nail unit melanoma, associated with high-risk human papillomavirus, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation or trauma, prior radiation, and older age, and often multifocal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — periungual disease may be multifocal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which clinical forms of Kaposi sarcoma does the deck describe?","choices":["Superficial spreading, lentigo maligna, nodular and acral lentiginous disease, each occurring in a different population and each with a different growth pattern and course","Patch, plaque, tumour and erythrodermic disease, each occurring in a different population and each representing a successive stage of the same underlying process","Classic in older men with a chronic course; endemic, often aggressive in young Black men in equatorial Africa; iatrogenic with immunosuppressive therapy; and epidemic in acquired immunodeficiency","Superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform disease, each occurring in a different population and each with a different histologic behaviour and course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — four forms, each with a different population and course. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which indications does the deck give for Mohs surgery in basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Eyelids, nasolabial folds, canthi, external ear and temple; recurrent tumours; a tissue-sparing or cosmetic need; and low-risk histology — superficial or nodular disease","The trunk, back, chest and shoulders; first presentations only; a purely cosmetic need; and any pigmented lesion whatever its histology","Any lesion in a patient over 65; any lesion larger than 6 mm; any lesion present more than a year; and any lesion the patient finds unsightly","Eyelids, nasolabial folds, canthi, external ear and temple; recurrent tumours; a tissue-sparing or cosmetic need; and aggressive histology — morpheaform, micronodular or infiltrative"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — four groupings, with the histologic subtypes named. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is preferred for invasive squamous cell carcinoma, and what is used for advanced or metastatic disease?","choices":["Radiation therapy alone for invasive disease; curettage and electrodesiccation for advanced or metastatic disease","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery for invasive disease; programmed death 1 blockade and cetuximab for advanced or metastatic disease","Imiquimod or topical fluorouracil for invasive disease; surgical excision for advanced or metastatic disease","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery for invasive disease; hedgehog pathway inhibitors for advanced or metastatic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — advanced disease needs specialist multidisciplinary care. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the early morphology of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, and what is it commonly mistaken for?","choices":["Localised pearly papules with telangiectasias, usually on the face and frequently smaller than 5 mm; it may resemble sebaceous hyperplasia","Red or purple macules and nodules, usually on the lower limbs and oral mucosa; it may resemble bacillary angiomatosis","Rough sandpaper papules on sun-exposed skin, usually under 6 mm; it may resemble seborrheic keratosis","Localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques, usually on the trunk and frequently larger than 5 cm; it may resemble psoriasis, eczema or tinea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why diagnosis is often delayed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"At what Breslow thickness is sentinel lymph node biopsy offered or discussed, and what lowers that threshold?","choices":["At 0.8 mm or greater; or at 1.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion","At 4.0 mm or greater; or at 2.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration or high mitotic rate","At any thickness once the diagnosis is confirmed, since thickness does not influence the decision to stage the nodes","At 1.0 mm or greater; or at 0.8 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three risk factors lower the threshold. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"}]};
